course: dictionary
lesson: 7544
title: Making a prediction
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A prediction is a guess about what will happen in the future. A good prediction is not a wild guess: it is based on clues, facts or evidence you already have. «A prediction is a sensible guess about what will happen next, based on clues.»
explain: Look at the clues before you guess. In a story, a character who packs a torch and a map is probably going somewhere dark, so you can predict they will explore a cave. «A wrong prediction is still useful, because it teaches you something.»
explain: In science, pupils make a prediction before a test: «I predict the ice will melt faster in the sun.» Then they check what really happens. The weather forecast is a prediction too. Useful phrases are make a prediction, my prediction is, and test your prediction.
fact: A prediction is a guess about what will happen in the future.
fact: A good prediction is based on facts, evidence or clues you already know.
fact: «Wild guesses with no link to the text, like a dragon turning into a cake for no reason, are not good predictions.»
fact: «Prediction» can also mean the act of saying what will happen.
fact: A wrong prediction still teaches you something, so test it and learn from the result.
exercise: The ice cube sits on a sunny windowsill. Lily says, «I predict it will melt first.» Which reason makes this a sensible prediction? | The ice cube will sing a song | The ice cube will turn into a dragon | The ice cube will grow into a tree | *The sun is hot, so the ice is likely to melt
exercise: «Wild guesses with no link to the text, like a dragon turning into a cake for no reason, are not good predictions.» What makes a guess a poor prediction? | It is written in a notebook | It is longer than one sentence | It has no link to clues or facts | *It has no link to clues or facts, so it is random
exercise: Mum says, «My prediction is that it will rain by lunchtime.» What is her prediction? | *A guess that rain will come by lunchtime | A recipe for soup | A list of lunch foods | Rain has already stopped
test: Why is a wrong prediction still useful? | It shows that the guess was perfect | It proves the facts were wrong | It means you should never guess again | *It teaches you something new
test: Which is a sensible prediction? Maya sees dark clouds and a strong wind. | She predicts the clouds will sing a song | She predicts the sky will be sunny all day | She predicts the wind will stop blowing forever | *She predicts it may rain soon
exam: Zara reads, «The hikers packed torches, rope and a map, so I predict they are going into the cave.» What is Zara making? | A fact about caves that is always true | A story about a dragon | A wild guess with no link to the text | *A sensible prediction based on clues
